<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="ch-planner" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mathematics four-year planner</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Check off the courses the student has completed, choose an emphasis if they
      have one, and set the next quarter to plan. The planner fills the remaining
      quarters with the courses still needed for the major, respecting every
      prerequisite and co-requisite in the Bulletin, the quarters each course is
      offered, and the alternate-year rotation. It then checks the plan against
      the degree requirements and lists anything at risk.
      For comfortable use, <url href="external/planner.html" visual="">open the planner full size in a new tab</url>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-planner">
    <title>The planner</title>

    <interactive xml:id="planner" iframe="planner.html" width="100%" aspect="2:3">
      <instructions>
        <p>
          Entries are kept in the browser, so an advisor can set up a student, close
          the page, and come back to it. <q>Clear student</q> resets everything except
          the offerings table. <q>Download plan (PDF)</q> opens the browser's print
          dialog with only the plan on the page: a header naming the emphasis and the
          completed courses, the four year-tables, the warnings, and the requirement
          check. Choose <q>Save as PDF</q> there to keep a copy.
        </p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <p>Interactive four-year planner (available in the HTML version).</p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-planner-how">
    <title>How the plan is built</title>

    <p>
      The planner starts from the set of courses the student still needs: the
      lower-division core, the emphasis courses, one course from each required
      upper-division category, any electives the student asked for, and enough
      further electives to reach seven upper-division courses. For each of these it
      computes the <term>latest workable quarter</term>: the last quarter in which
      the course is offered that still leaves room for everything that depends on
      it. A course whose latest workable quarter has already passed is reported as
      <em>blocked</em>, with the chain that caused it.
    </p>

    <p>
      It then walks forward from the next quarter to plan. In each quarter it
      takes the courses whose prerequisites are met, that are offered that quarter,
      and that fit under the per-quarter cap, choosing the most urgent first:
      earliest latest-workable-quarter, then alternate-year courses, then
      lower-division courses. Pre-or-co-requisites (<c>MATH 122</c> with
      <c>MATH 14</c>, <c>MATH 123</c> with <c>MATH 53</c>) may be scheduled in the
      same quarter as the course that needs them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Three kinds of warning appear under the tables.
    </p>
    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Blocked</title>
        <p>
          A course can no longer be completed by Year 4 Spring, either because an
          alternate-year course has no remaining offering or because a chain such as
          <c>MATH 52</c> <m>\to</m> <c>MATH 111</c> <m>\to</m> <c>MATH 112</c> has run
          out of quarters.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Cannot wait</title>
        <p>
          A course within the next year that has no later slot in the plan, with the
          reason: the courses that must follow it, or the fact that it is not offered
          again before graduation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Note</title>
        <p>
          Advice that does not change the plan, such as the deadline for declaring
          an emphasis (end of junior year) and the fact that <c>MATH 195</c> satisfies
          the ELSJ requirement but does not count toward the seven upper-division
          courses.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>

    <p>
      The plan shows only major courses. Quarters marked
      <q>core / electives</q> have no major course scheduled and are where Core
      Curriculum and free electives go.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-planner-offerings">
    <title>Course offerings</title>
    <p>
      The Bulletin marks a course as <q>offered in alternate years</q> but does not
      say which quarter or which years. The planner's offerings table, at the bottom
      of the page, holds placeholders chosen so that sequences like
      <c>MATH 111</c>/<c>112</c> and <c>MATH 153</c>/<c>154</c> fall in consecutive
      quarters of the same year. Edit the table to match the department's schedule;
      the plan and its warnings update at once. Edits persist in the browser only,
      so once the rotation is settled the defaults in <c>assets/planner.html</c>
      (the <c>off</c> and <c>alt</c> fields of the course table) should be changed
      to match, so that every visitor sees the real schedule.
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>
